{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>See, I have called by name Betzalel son of Uri son of Hur.</b> HaShem said to Moses, \"See, I have called by name,\" and Moses said to Israel, \"See, HaShem has called by name\" (Exodus 35:30). The reason is that Israel in Egypt were crushed in the labor of mortar and bricks; they did not learn the craft of silver and gold and the working of precious stones, nor did they see them at all. And so it is a wonder that there should be found among them a man great in wisdom in silver and in gold and in the working of stone and wood, and in weaving and embroidery and weaving — for even among those who study before sages, one will not be found proficient in all the crafts together, and those who know them and are accustomed to them, whose hands are constantly in clay and mud, cannot produce with them fine and beautiful craftsmanship. Moreover, he is great in wisdom, in understanding, and in knowledge, to comprehend the secret of the Tabernacle and all its vessels — why they were commanded and to what they allude.\n\nTherefore HaShem said to Moses that he should see this wonder, and know that He filled him with the spirit of Elohim to know all these things, in order that he might make the Tabernacle — for it was the will before Him to make the Tabernacle in the wilderness, and for its honor He created him, for He calls the generations from the beginning (Isaiah 41:4), in the manner of \"Before I formed you in the womb I knew you, and before you came forth from the womb I consecrated you\" (Jeremiah 1:5). And in this same idiom (Exodus 16:29): \"See that HaShem has given you the Sabbath; therefore He gives you on the sixth day bread for two days.\"\n\nAnd so our Rabbis have a midrash on this (Exodus Rabbah 40:2): He showed him the book of the first man and said to him, \"Each one I prepared from that hour, and Betzalel too from that hour I prepared him,\" as it is said, \"See, I have called by name Betzalel\" — and this is like the matter that I explained. And they further said (Berakhot 55a): Betzalel knew how to combine the letters with which heaven and earth were created. And the meaning is that the Tabernacle alludes to these, and he is the one who knows and understands its secret:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The paragraph break marked by the source's <br> tag corresponds to a shift in Ramban's argument from the practical wonder to the theological explanation and rabbinic sources; preserved here as a newline break."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:19:39.734146197Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}